    Aa({'type': 'h1', 'text': '5. Discussion'})
    Aa({'type': 'h2', 'text': '5.1. What the two protocols measure'})
    P("A random split asks whether a model can classify further samples from sources it has seen; a grouped split asks whether what it has learned about a setting carries over to other sources. A user of a published classifier faces the second question. The difference is not specific to an algorithm: it appears for boosting, bagging, kernel and neighbour-based models, is smallest for low-capacity models, which cannot adapt to individual suites, and can be reproduced inside the random protocol.")
    P("Our grouped figures are estimates with wide intervals and biases in both directions. They are pessimistic in that withholding a large source removes far more of a small class than a random fifth, up to a third of all OPB analyses. They are optimistic in that reference codes are imperfect groups: composite codes and compilations leave nearly a third of the test samples linked to a training sample through a shared reference, and different publications describe the same volcano or basin. Grouping by locality would be preferable. Resampling cannot remove the near-confounding of setting, database and probably analysed material: differences between glass and whole-rock analyses could be learned as tectonic signal.")
    Aa({'type': 'h2', 'text': '5.2. Geological reading of the errors'})
    P("The classes that transfer best, MORB, CFB and OIB, are also the largest, so their advantage is partly one of sample size and residual linkage. The classes that transfer worst are those for which a gradational or heterogeneous composition is expected. Back-arc basin basalts range from MORB-like to arc-like compositions as the subduction component increases (Pearce and Stern, 2006). The misclassified BABB samples follow this order: those assigned to MORB have MORB-like Ba/Nb, Th/Nb and Nb/La, and those assigned to arc classes have arc-like values (Section 4.4). The models thus place a back-arc sample consistently on the ridge-to-arc spectrum, whereas the label records the basin, and the databases themselves disagree on where intra-oceanic arc ends and back-arc begins (Section 2).")
    P("For oceanic plateau basalts the result is decided by a handful of sources, and here the errors are only partly orderly: OPB analyses with high La/Yb are assigned to CFB or OIB, the classes whose enrichment they share, but those assigned to MORB have the same low La/Yb as those classified correctly. Most plateau lavas are thus close to ridge basalts in La/Yb (cf. Xia and Li, 2019), and once duplicated samples are set aside the models recognise fewer than half of them. The arc classes illustrate a different problem. The continental-arc class has a median Nb/La close to that of flood basalts (Section 2), so many of its samples lack the signature on which arc discrimination rests, while a share of flood basalts carries an arc-like signature, commonly attributed to lithospheric mantle or crustal contributions (Xia and Li, 2019).")
    P("Nb, Th, Ba and Sr are the elements whose ratios define the subduction signature (Pearce and Peate, 1995; Pearce, 2008), and four of the ten ratios selected in every fold set Nb or Ta against Th, Ba or La. Sr and Ba are also sensitive to alteration and to plagioclase accumulation, so part of their weight may reflect the material analysed. That the alteration-resistant feature set seems to lose more under grouped validation than the full set is a caution for the altered and ancient rocks to which discrimination is most often applied.")
    Aa({'type': 'h2', 'text': '5.3. Responses that need no new data'})
